# build artifacts the scripts/build_ecoli_pangenome_*.sh tutorials write into the
# repo root (ecoli_pangenome_build/, ecoli_cactus_ava.paf, ...). Anchored:
# unanchored it also swallowed website/static/img/**/ecoli_*.png, so a new
# tutorial figure silently never got committed.
/ecoli_*

# Same, for scripts/build_1000g_cnv_zarr.sh. The 1000 Genomes copy-number store
# is hosted at jbrowse.org/demos/1000g and read from there by
# test_data/1000g_cnv/config.json — it used to be 20 committed chunk files, and
# a zarr chunk key is named from its position, so every rebuild rewrote them all
# as fresh undeltifiable blobs. Publishing is an `aws s3 sync`, not a `git add`.
/1000g_cnv_build/

# Figure bytes live in the S3 figure store, not in git. `figures.lock` is the
# tracked record — one line per figure — and website/scripts/figure-store.ts
# explains the arrangement. Tracked, these cost 0.67 GiB of a 1.64 GiB pack and
# grew ~1.4 GiB/yr, because the weekly sweep rewrites whatever the app moved
# (4,918 figure revisions in six months) and every one is an undeltifiable blob
# every clone keeps forever.
#
# `pnpm figures:pull` installs them — plain HTTPS, no credentials, and `dev` and
# `build` run it for you. `pnpm figures:push` publishes what you regenerated and
# rewrites figures.lock; commit that, and its diff is the record of what moved.
#
# NO TRAILING SLASH, deliberately. A trailing slash matches directories only,
# and the documented way to give a worktree the machine's figures is to SYMLINK
# these two paths at the primary checkout's (CLAUDE.md). A symlink is not a
# directory, so it was not ignored, and a `git add -A` in that worktree committed
# an absolute path — /home/<user>/src/... — that replaces the figure directory
# with a dangling link on every other machine. Without the slash the path is
# ignored whatever it is, which is what the instruction to make the link assumes.
website/static/img
products/jbrowse-img/img

# The media store, same arrangement for the same reason: bytes in S3
# (jb2-media), git tracking website/media.lock, `pnpm media:pull` installing what
# it names and `build` running it. A screencast is a worse offender than a
# figure — re-filming the same tour produces different bytes every time, so every
# regen is a new undeltifiable blob. website/scripts/media.ts is the CLI, and
# says why the docs deploy needs the pull.
website/static/media
scripts/__pycache__
**/.env
**/node_modules/
**/.vscode/
yarn-error.log
*.swp
*.swo
.DS_Store
lint.log
.eslintcache
.idea
**/umd
yalc.lock
.yalc
**/package-lock.json
**/__diff_output__/
# Raster dumps some tests write for eyeballing. Nothing compares them, so a
# tracked copy just goes stale and leaves the worktree permanently dirty — which
# in a shared worktree means it rides along into someone else's commit. Anything
# a test actually asserts belongs in __snapshots__/ or __image_snapshots__/.
**/__debug_output__/
**/dist/
**/esm/
**/nextstrain-demos-data/
